Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Prisms Chew the Gale
- Story & Context: During a force-ten blizzard at Cape Svinøy that shattered three exterior catwalk panes, keeper Magnus Lind locked himself in the transmitter cabin, running his wooden clarinet straight into an overdriven four-track tape deck while looping a distorted 1973 Italian symphonic rock bassline. The resulting transmission bewildered a Swedish container ship when Lind’s brother shouted verse rhymes through the fog horn mic, creating the genre's first acknowledged bootleg hit. "We turn the brass ring till the salt eats through," snarled across the nautical frequencies, blending mechanical maritime panic with ferocious inner-city battle rap intensity.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Macro close-up of a cracked lighthouse prism lens glistening with freezing sea spray, light splintering into sharp violet and rust-orange beams, while the silhouette of a hooded figure holding a microphone looms in the frozen glass reflection, dark gothic maritime album art, hyper-detailed textures.

Track 2: Cullet on the Tape Head
- Story & Context: Astrid Dahl composed this confession in the damp basement of the Skomvær foghorn shed, surrounded by buckets of cullet—broken shards of green glass salvaged from shattered storm lanterns—which she physically scraped across the open reels of an Akai recorder to generate percussive treble hiss. The track captured her spiraling cabin fever and bitter resentment toward the coastal administration, who had threatened to seize her radio equipment after discovering she was using distress channels to broadcast minor-key boom-bap suites. In her trademark gravelly delivery, the hook declared, "I trade my rations for an eighth-note snap," over a serpentine, Phrygian bass clarinet groove that mimicked the groaning of ice against stone.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Broken emerald and sea-glass fragments scattered across a vintage reel-to-reel magnetic tape machine, illuminated by a single warm candle flame in a dark damp stone cellar, Moody gothic lighting, deep shadows, cinematic macro realism.

Track 3: Did the Lead Came Hold?
- Story & Context: Long after the Jan Mayen beacon was automated and stripped of its crew, trawler crews crossing the Barents Sea claimed their VHF monitors would still pick up phantom broadcasts whenever winter temperatures plunged past thirty below. Legend holds that the final keeper spent his last night soldering stained-glass fragments into the radio mast, reciting verse after verse over an eleven-minute symphonic drum suite that never resolved to its root chord. The closing refrain, "The beam went cold but the rhythm stayed alive," became a mythic epitaph spray-painted along harbor sea walls from Tromsø to Reykjavik.
